^SAMPLE = GSM4002810
!Sample_title = Control4
!Sample_geo_accession = GSM4002810
!Sample_status = Public on Feb 12 2020
!Sample_submission_date = Aug 02 2019
!Sample_last_update_date = Feb 13 2020
!Sample_type = SRA
!Sample_channel_count = 1
!Sample_source_name_ch1 = CNS Percoll pellet
!Sample_organism_ch1 = Homo sapiens
!Sample_taxid_ch1 = 9606
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = disease: Healthy control tissue; glioblastoma patient
!Sample_growth_protocol_ch1 = N/A
!Sample_molecule_ch1 = total RNA
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = For samples 1-3: Tissue was mechanically dissociated, treated with 0.25% trypsin and 25ug/mL Dnase for 30 min at 37C, and then passed through a 140um nylon mesh. The resulting cells were then separated on 30% percoll gradient to remove myelin and erythrocytes. For samples 4-5: A cell suspension was generated by papain digestion, neutralization, and 70um filtration of cell extract. Cells were centrifuged, resuspended in 30% Percoll, centrifuged, washed in PBS, and cell density was quantified. Cells were suspended in PBS and 16% Opti-prep.
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = For samples 1-3: Libraries were created according to the 10X Genomics protocol (Zheng et al. Nature Communications 2017). For samples 4-5: Libraries were created according to the Drop-seq protocol in Macosko et al. Cell 2015. Following reverse transcription, exonuclease I treatment, and PCR, cDNA libraries were quantified and 600 pg of cDNA was tagmented using Nextera XT library prep kit.
!Sample_data_processing = Raw sequencing data were processed using the 10x Genomics Cell Ranger pipeline (version 1.3).
!Sample_data_processing = Cellranger mkfastq demultiplexed libraries based on sample indices and generated FASTQ files
!Sample_data_processing = Cellranger count performed alignment against hg38 human genome using STAR , filtering, and feature counting for unique molecular identifier (UMI)
!Sample_data_processing = Genome_build: hg38
!Sample_data_processing = Supplementary_files_format_and_content: csv format with rows as genes and columns as cells
